## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical end-to-end testing of a distributed MongoDB search feature, focusing on ranking accuracy, fuzziness robustness, pagination, performance KPIs, and resilience under failover and repartitioning. It also emphasizes realistic CI integration and data generation strategies.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end search tests for ranking and fuzziness
- Performance metrics: p95 latency, throughput, error rate
- Resilience: replica failover, shard migration, partitions
- Data modeling: synthetic boosts and realistic term distributions
- CI data management and flaky-test handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch (pseudo)
async function testSearchRanking() {
  const res = await api.search('coffee', { fuzziness: 1, page: 1, perPage: 10 })
  if (res.results.length !== 10) throw new Error('unexpected length')
  if (res.results[0].score < res.results[1].score) throw new Error('ranking not sorted')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ranking under load spikes and index churn?
- How would you detect and isolate flaky tests caused by replication lag?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first behavior are core for reliability but beginners often struggle with storage APIs and network conditions. This question isolates those concepts in a tangible, front-end context.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first strategy
- IndexedDB usage
- Sync mechanics and idempotence
- Conflict resolution policy
- CI network condition emulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for offline caching test
describe('Offline-first sync', () => {
  it('caches submissions offline and syncs when online', () => {
    cy.visit('/form');
    cy.window().then(w => { w.navigator.onLine = false; });
    cy.get('#name').type('Alice');
    cy.get('#email').type('a@example.com');
    cy.get('#submit').click();
    // verify IndexedDB contains 1 entry
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for different storage backends (IndexedDB vs LocalStorage)?
- How would you measure flakiness and improve CI stability?

